  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 932 AM MDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nStarting today and through Saturday, the borderland will be trending \ntoward a mostly dry and warmer pattern as high pressure builds aloft \nover the the desert southwest. Northeasterly flow surrounding the\nhigh will bring in dry, continental air from the Rockies. While \nsufficient moisture remains for lowland thunderstorm chances \ntoday, warmer temperatures aloft will reduce instability for the \nlowlands and limit thunderstorm development to mainly the area \nmountains for Friday and Saturday. Friday and Saturday are also \nforecast to be the warmest days of the week for the region, with \nseveral lowland locations running north of 10 degrees above \naverage both days--the lower Rio Grande Valley may even reach the \ntriple digit mark.\n\nThe weather pattern will become more active starting Sunday as a \nlarge low pressure system moving inland from the Pacific northwest \nwill push the upper-level high eastward. This will set up a moist \nsoutherly flow aloft that will facilitate widespread thunderstorm \ndevelopment across the area for several days--how much rain the \nlowlands will get is unclear at this time. Increased moisture and \nrain chances will result in lower temperatures closer to seasonal \naverages. The long term outlook for late next week remains uncertain \nwith long range forecast models split on whether the moisture and \nthunderstorm chances continue or we trend back to a drier weather \npattern.",
